-- title:
--   Stabilizer degree budget using finite chart jet generators
-- statement:
--   This is the previous stabilizer cubic-chart degree-budget theorem with only its budget structure replaced by FiniteChartJetBudget. The data and all hypotheses concerning the period pair, sigma data, entire coordinates, nonzero pullback, high-order zeros on the triple sumset, and quasiperiod map are unchanged. It must still produce a nonempty polynomially constrained locus W, an exponent b <= 2, and natural-number budgets indexed by the represented stabilizer cosets, with the original uniform sum inequality.
--
--   The candidate ideals now have the finite displayed generators F_c, Q_c, D_c Q_c, ..., D_c^T Q_c at each required order T. The completed supporting theorem identifies these with the previous differential prolongations exactly. A checked converse shows equivalence of the old and new budget formulations, preserving the chart, point, candidate-prime tests and localized lengths.
--
--   The unresolved work is the global geometric selection and uniform total multiplicity/degree bound. A generic degree bound max(3,m+2n+T) for the individual generators still depends on the derivative order, and applying it directly does not yield a constant independent of U. The numerical A.1 target is unchanged. No global scheme-gluing or coset/component selection theorem is asserted here.
-- source:
--   Philippon (1986), Lemmes de zeros dans les groupes algebriques commutatifs, Bull. Soc. Math. France 114, 355-383, section 5, pp. 380-382: derivative and translation ideals in the zero-estimate argument. https://www.numdam.org/item/10.24033/bsmf.2060.pdf. Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, Theorem A.2. https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. The new algebraic step proves that each order-T prolongation of the explicit cubic-plus-Q chart base is generated by the cubic and the normalized Q jets through order T, preserving exact ideals and lengths. The individual jet-degree bounds were already available and are reused. The common total-degree bound max(3,m+2*n+T) depends on T; it does not prove the uniform total multiplicity budget. Geometric locus selection, coset/component control and the uniform degree estimate remain Open.

